/**
\page parameters Parameter reference

Every argument accepted by every sampler, in one place. Defaults are the values you get by
omitting the argument; violating a stated range throws `std::invalid_argument` from
`define(...)` rather than failing later.

\tableofcontents

\section params_shared Conventions shared by all samplers

\subsection params_theta Thermal speeds: `theta_perp`, `theta_par`

`theta` is a **thermal speed**, not a temperature, and the convention is fixed by

    Vx ~ N(0, theta_perp^2 / 2)

for the bi-Maxwellian, and by `v = sqrt(kappa) * theta * u` for the bi-kappa (see
\ref params_bikappa). If you are converting from a temperature, the factor of `1/2` matters:
`theta = sqrt(2*k_B*T/m)`, not `sqrt(k_B*T/m)`.

`theta_perp` sets both directions transverse to `ub`; `theta_par` sets the direction along it.
Isotropic is `theta_perp == theta_par`. Both must be **strictly positive**; zero throws rather
than degenerating to a delta function.

\subsection params_ub Field direction: `ub`

A 3-vector giving the local magnetic-field direction, as `{bx, by, bz}`.

- **It does not need to be normalized.** It is normalized internally, so `{0,0,2}` and `{0,0,1}`
  behave identically, and `{1,1,1}` is a legal 45-degree field.
- **It must be non-zero.** A zero vector throws `std::invalid_argument`.
- **`{0,0,1}` is a fast path**, not a special meaning: the sampler recognizes it and skips the
  rotation entirely. The result is the same as any other unit vector along z.

Samples are generated in the field-aligned frame and rotated into your global frame before being
returned, so the returned `{vx, vy, vz}` are always in the simulation frame.

\note The perpendicular axes are chosen internally and are not part of the interface. For an
axisymmetric distribution this is unobservable. It becomes observable only under a velocity cap,
whose box is tied to those axes — see \ref usage_cap_frame.

\subsection params_seed Seeding: `seed`

An `int`, accepted as the last argument of `define(...)` or passed to `seed(...)` at any time.

| Value | Effect |
| ----- | ------ |
| `>= 0` | Deterministic. The same seed reproduces the same sequence byte for byte. |
| `< 0`  | Reseeds from `std::random_device`, i.e. unpredictable. |

Omitting it leaves the generator seeded from `std::random_device` at construction, so **runs are
not reproducible unless you seed them**. Re-seeding restarts the stream; it does not continue it.

To drive several samplers from one stream instead, pass your own generator to `operator()` —
see \ref usage_external_rng.

\subsection params_rejection Rejection-sampler knobs: `probe_points`, `max_reject_tries`

These apply to the three samplers that take a user-supplied function
(`general_velocity_generator`, `field_aligned_velocity_generator`,
`general_position_generator`). You can usually ignore them; this section is what to read when a
sampler throws or when the output looks wrong.

| Parameter | Meaning | Defaults |
| --------- | ------- | -------- |
| `probe_points` | Number of grid points used once, at `define(...)` time, to estimate the maximum of your function. That maximum (times a 5% margin) becomes the rejection envelope. | `1024` for the velocity generators, `4096` for the position generator |
| `max_reject_tries` | How many candidate draws a single sample may consume before the sampler gives up and throws `std::runtime_error`. | `100000` for the velocity generators, `200000` for the position generator |

\warning **`probe_points` can silently bias your output.** The envelope is estimated on a uniform
grid. If your function has a peak narrow enough to fall between two probe points, the estimated
maximum is too low, and every candidate in that peak is accepted unconditionally — the sampled
distribution is then wrong, with no exception thrown and no warning printed. If your `g(w)` or
`rho(x)` has a narrow spike, raise `probe_points` until `pdfUpperBound()` stops increasing.

Raising `max_reject_tries` treats the symptom of a badly matched envelope, not the cause: a
sampler that exhausts it is usually one whose function is tiny over most of its domain. Narrowing
the domain (`w_min`, `w_max`, or the position bounds) is the effective fix, and it is faster too.

\section params_bikappa bi_kappa_distribution

    define(kappa, theta_perp, theta_par, ub, max_normalized_velocity, seed)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `kappa` | Spectral index. Small `kappa` means heavy suprathermal tails; `kappa -> infinity` recovers the bi-Maxwellian. | `2.0` | `> 0.5` (strictly; `0.5` throws) |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed transverse to `ub`. See \ref params_theta. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `theta_par` | Thermal speed along `ub`. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | `{0,0,1}` | non-zero |
| `max_normalized_velocity` | Velocity cap in thermal speeds, or `no_cap()`. See \ref params_cap. | `no_cap()` (**off**) | `> 0`, or `no_cap()` |
| `seed` | See \ref params_seed. | from `std::random_device` | any `int` |

**What `kappa` means for moments.** Moments of order `l` exist only for `kappa > (l+1)/2`. The
distribution is perfectly samplable for any `kappa > 0.5`, but for `0.5 < kappa <= 1.5` it has
**no finite variance**, so a temperature computed from your loaded particles will not converge as
you add more of them — it will wander. That is a property of the distribution you asked for, not
a bug in the sampler. Below `kappa = 1.5`, use quantiles rather than moments to characterize what
you loaded.

**Operating range.** There are two separate limits here and releases before 2.0.0 conflated
them.

The first is arithmetic and was avoidable. The radius is `R = sqrt(X1)/sqrt(X2)` with
`X2 ~ Gamma(kappa - 1/2, 1)`, so as `kappa` approaches `1/2` the denominator's shape
approaches zero and `X2` underflows to exactly zero long before the mathematical variate is
negligible. Release 1.0.0 lost those draws — 2.4 % of them at `kappa = 0.505` in `double` —
which is why its documented range was `kappa >= 0.55` in `double` and `>= 0.75` in `float`.
Release 2.0.0 never forms `X2`; it carries `log X2` and decides representability in the log
domain, and that mode is gone.

The second is a property of the floating-point type and cannot be removed by any sampler.
Sufficiently near `kappa = 1/2` the bi-Kappa law puts non-zero probability outside every
finite range, and those velocities exist as mathematics without existing as numbers. The
probability is exactly

    P(overflow) = [Gamma(3/2+a) / (Gamma(3/2) Gamma(a+1))] * MAX^(-2a)
                  * E[(sqrt(kappa) max_j |n_j|)^(2a)],        a = kappa - 1/2

for the isotropic, unrotated case. It falls away extremely fast: in `double` it is 8.3e-4 at
`kappa = 0.505`, 6.8e-7 at `0.51`, and 1.5e-31 at `0.55`; in `float`, 1.4e-4 at `0.55`,
1.9e-8 at `0.60` and 5.3e-20 at `0.75`. Such draws are returned with non-finite components
and counted by n_nonfinite(), which is the honest answer rather than a silent redraw.

So for practical purposes `double` is unrestricted above `kappa = 0.51` and `float` above
`kappa = 0.6`; below that, expect a measurable fraction of non-finite returns, know that it
is the type's limit and not the sampler's, and check the counter. The derivation and its
empirical check are in `experiments/exp7_confirmatory/config/honest_floor.md`.

\section params_cap The velocity cap

`max_normalized_velocity` (call it `lam`) accepts a finite positive value or the sentinel
`no_cap()`. It is the one parameter that changes **which distribution you sample**, not merely
its shape, so it has its own section: \ref usage_bikappa_cap. It works identically on
`bi_kappa_distribution` and `bi_maxwellian_distribution`.

In brief:

- `no_cap()` (`= +infinity`, and the default on both samplers) samples the named distribution,
  with no rejection loop at all.
- A finite `lam` samples the bi-kappa distribution *conditioned on* the box
  `|v_x|/theta_perp <= lam`, `|v_y|/theta_perp <= lam`, `|v_par|/theta_par <= lam` — a different,
  bounded law whose tails and moments differ from the one you named.
- `param().capped()` tells you which is in force. Passing `0` to mean "off" throws; pass
  `no_cap()`.

\section params_bimaxwellian bi_maxwellian_distribution

    define(theta_perp, theta_par, ub, max_normalized_velocity, seed)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed transverse to `ub`. See \ref params_theta. | *required* | `> 0` |
| `theta_par` | Thermal speed along `ub`. | `1.0` | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | `{0,0,1}` | non-zero |
| `max_normalized_velocity` | Velocity cap in thermal speeds, or `no_cap()`. See \ref params_cap. | `no_cap()` (**off**) | `> 0`, or `no_cap()` |
| `seed` | See \ref params_seed. | from `std::random_device` | any `int` |

The cap behaves exactly as it does on `bi_kappa_distribution`: off by default, `no_cap()` as the
sentinel, `param().capped()` as the query, and no rejection loop at all when uncapped. Gaussian
tails are thin enough that a realistic cap is never exercised by a finite sample, so on this
sampler the choice rarely changes what you load — but it still changes which law you asked for,
and the two classes now answer that question the same way.

\section params_generalvelocity general_velocity_generator

    define(speed_sq_pdf, speed_sq_min, speed_sq_max, probe_points, max_reject_tries)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `speed_sq_pdf` | Your function `g(w)`, whose argument is the **speed squared**, not the speed. Need not be normalized — any non-negative function proportional to the density will do. | *required* | callable, non-negative, finite, positive somewhere in range |
| `speed_sq_min` | Lower end of the sampled `w` range. | *required* | `>= 0` |
| `speed_sq_max` | Upper end. | *required* | `> speed_sq_min` |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `1024` | `> 1` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `100000` | `> 0` |

The direction is isotropic on the sphere and the speed is `sqrt(w)`. Because the argument is
`|v|^2`, a Maxwellian in speed is `g(w) = exp(-w/theta^2)` — writing `exp(-v^2/theta^2)` with `v`
meaning speed gives the same thing, but writing a function of speed *linearly* will not.

`speed_sq_min` and `speed_sq_max` are a **hard support**, not a hint: nothing outside them is
ever produced. Choose `speed_sq_max` to cover the tail you care about, since anything past it is
silently absent rather than rejected.

\section params_fieldaligned field_aligned_velocity_generator

    define(speed_sq_pdf, speed_sq_min, speed_sq_max, theta_perp, ub, parallel_sign,
           probe_points, max_reject_tries)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `speed_sq_pdf` | Your `g(w)` for the **parallel** component, where `w = v_par^2`. | *required* | as above |
| `speed_sq_min` | Lower end of the sampled `v_par^2`. | *required* | `>= 0` |
| `speed_sq_max` | Upper end. | *required* | `> speed_sq_min` |
| `theta_perp` | Thermal speed of the two perpendicular components, which are Maxwellian: each `~ N(0, theta_perp^2/2)`. | *required* | `> 0` |
| `ub` | Field direction. See \ref params_ub. | *required* | non-zero |
| `parallel_sign` | `+1` sends the parallel component along `ub`, `-1` sends it opposite. | *required* | exactly `+1` or `-1` |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `1024` | `> 1` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `100000` | `> 0` |

`parallel_sign` makes this a **one-sided** distribution: every sample streams the same way along
the field. It is meant for beams and drifting populations. For a symmetric parallel distribution,
draw the sign yourself, or use `bi_kappa_distribution` / `bi_maxwellian_distribution`.

\section params_generalposition general_position_generator

    define(dimension, lower_bounds, upper_bounds, density_function, probe_points,
           max_reject_tries)

| Parameter | Meaning | Default | Valid range |
| --------- | ------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `dimension` | How many spatial dimensions to sample. | *required* | `1`, `2`, or `3` |
| `lower_bounds` | Lower corner of the sampling box, as a `std::vector<Real>`. | *required* | size must equal `dimension` |
| `upper_bounds` | Upper corner. | *required* | size must equal `dimension`, each above its lower |
| `density_function` | Your `rho(x)`, taking a `std::vector<Real>` of length `dimension`. Need not be normalized. | *required* | callable, non-negative, finite, positive somewhere |
| `probe_points` | See \ref params_rejection. | `4096` | `> 0` |
| `max_reject_tries` | See \ref params_rejection. | `200000` | `> 0` |

The returned point is a `std::vector<Real>` of length `dimension`, not a fixed-size array — this
is the one sampler whose `point_type` is not `std::array<Real,3>`.

\section params_errors What throws, and when

| Exception | Thrown by | Cause |
| --------- | --------- | ----- |
| `std::invalid_argument` | `define(...)` | Any range violation in the tables above: `kappa <= 0.5`, non-positive `theta` or cap, `speed_sq_min >= speed_sq_max`, negative `speed_sq_min`, zero `ub`, `parallel_sign` other than ±1, `dimension` outside 1–3, mismatched bounds sizes, a null callable. |
| `std::runtime_error` | `operator()` | A `general_*` or `field_aligned_*` generator called before `define(...)`; or a rejection loop that exhausted `max_reject_tries`; or your function returned a negative or non-finite value. |

Validation happens entirely in `define(...)`, so a sampler that constructed cleanly will not throw
on a parameter later — the individual setters (`kappa(...)`, `theta_par(...)`, …) re-run the full
validation, so they can throw at the point of the change instead.
*/
